1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.310
Case Study:

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.425
Distributed Key-Value Store.

3
00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:07.971
Chapter 54, from the Case Studies part of the System Design Playbook.

4
00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:10.681
At a glance:

5
00:00:10.961 --> 00:00:12.135
Chapter at a glance.

6
00:00:12.415 --> 00:00:13.417
Reader bands:

7
00:00:13.697 --> 00:00:15.980
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:26.265
Ch 10 (quorums, LWW, CRDTs), Ch 11 (consistent hashing), Ch 12 (clocks, Raft), Ch 44 (caches).

9
00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:36.380
What you will build: the inside of a Dynamo or Cassandra-style store that could hold the platform's carts, sessions, order-status timelines and device tokens.

10
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:51.977
It has four parts: a storage engine that turns random writes into sequential ones;. a write-ahead log whose acknowledgements mean something;. anti-entropy that finds divergent replicas cheaply;. tunable consistency and its latency cost.

11
00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:58.170
Earlier chapters covered partitioning, quorum arithmetic and conflict resolution.

12
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.199
This chapter opens the box.

13
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:04.085
It is the design a key-value store interview question at depth.

14
00:01:04.365 --> 00:01:05.431
Lab results:

15
00:01:05.711 --> 00:01:08.335
L1: storage engines (real, on disk).

16
00:01:08.615 --> 00:01:18.151
Bitcask reads in 1 disk access (p50 2.8 µs, page-cached), but keeps 101 MB of keys in RAM.

17
00:01:18.431 --> 00:01:28.821
An LSM tree with 72 SSTables and no Bloom filters read 30 files per hit and 72 per miss (p99 1.3 ms).

18
00:01:30.171 --> 00:01:34.352
Bloom filters cut this to 1.24 and 0.59.

19
00:01:34.632 --> 00:01:42.995
Compaction cut space amplification from 1.91 times to 1.05 times and brought every read to 1 file.

20
00:01:43.275 --> 00:01:46.304
L2: write-ahead log versus a real SIGKILL.

21
00:01:46.584 --> 00:01:56.227
Acking from a user-space buffer lost 100 acknowledged writes. write()-then-ack lost none, but only survives a process crash, not a power loss.

22
00:01:57.577 --> 00:01:58.835
fsync per record:

23
00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:01.654
8,818 acks per second.

24
00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:02.851
Group commit:

25
00:02:03.131 --> 00:02:07.355
28,750 per second, with the same guarantee.

26
00:02:07.635 --> 00:02:08.489
L3:

27
00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:13.526
Merkle anti-entropy (real SHA-256, 2M keys).

28
00:02:13.806 --> 00:02:21.379
Finding 10 divergent keys took 13.7 KB instead of a 91.6 MB full comparison.

29
00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:24.689
Finding 1,000 took 0.9 MB.

30
00:02:24.969 --> 00:02:29.939
At 200,000 differences (10%), the saving mostly disappears.

31
00:02:30.219 --> 00:02:33.163
L4: tunable consistency (simulation).

32
00:02:34.513 --> 00:02:43.004
R=1 or W=1 returned the value from before the client's own acknowledged write ~3% of the time.

33
00:02:43.284 --> 00:02:54.036
R=2 or W=2 returned 0% stale reads, at a read p99 of 25.8 ms versus 3.0 ms.

34
00:02:54.316 --> 00:03:00.823
W=3 put every replica stall into the write p99 (194 ms).

35
00:03:01.103 --> 00:03:03.321
Hedging fixes tails, not staleness.

36
00:03:03.601 --> 00:03:04.583
Reading time:

37
00:03:04.863 --> 00:03:06.655
