38
00:03:08.005 --> 00:03:08.858
Part 1.

39
00:03:09.138 --> 00:03:11.058
Introduction and Why It Matters.

40
00:03:12.308 --> 00:03:14.335
A story: the carts that came back.

41
00:03:14.615 --> 00:03:22.785
The cart service moved from PostgreSQL to a self-managed, Dynamo-style key-value store for write throughput during sales.

42
00:03:23.065 --> 00:03:24.111
Within a quarter:

43
00:03:24.391 --> 00:03:26.119
Nodes kept running out of disk.

44
00:03:26.399 --> 00:03:28.959
Every cart update was a new version on disk.

45
00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:34.593
Compaction had been tuned down to save I or O during sales, so old versions piled up.

46
00:03:34.873 --> 00:03:38.692
One node hit 100% disk and stopped accepting writes.

47
00:03:38.972 --> 00:03:40.508
Removed items came back.

48
00:03:40.788 --> 00:03:42.559
A customer removed an item.

49
00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:48.684
The replica that took the write stalled, and the read went to another replica, which still had the old cart.

50
00:03:48.964 --> 00:03:51.737
That was R=1, W=1.

51
00:03:52.017 --> 00:03:54.471
The customer checked out with the removed item.

52
00:03:55.821 --> 00:03:58.103
Acknowledged writes vanished after a crash.

53
00:03:58.383 --> 00:04:00.517
A node was killed by the OOM killer.

54
00:04:00.797 --> 00:04:06.983
The client library had been configured to ack after the write reached the commit-log buffer, not after fsync.

55
00:04:07.263 --> 00:04:10.250
The last second of cart updates on that node disappeared.

56
00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.173
Repair took the cluster down.

57
00:04:12.453 --> 00:04:17.999
After a node was replaced, a full repair streamed entire token ranges between replicas.

58
00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.605
The network saturated during the evening peak.

59
00:04:21.955 --> 00:04:26.456
Key-value stores look simple (get, put), but each one is a small database.

60
00:04:26.736 --> 00:04:35.781
It has a storage engine with amplification trade-offs, a durability contract, replication with repair, and consistency you choose per request.

61
00:04:37.131 --> 00:04:37.814
Quote:

62
00:04:38.094 --> 00:04:39.758
The thesis of this chapter.

63
00:04:40.038 --> 00:04:44.731
Log-structured storage makes writes cheap and moves the cost to reads and space.

64
00:04:45.011 --> 00:04:51.454
Bloom filters and compaction pay it back, and compaction tuning is the core operational trade-off (L1).

65
00:04:51.734 --> 00:05:00.203
An acknowledgement is a durability promise: fsync, or group commit, before acking, and detect torn records on recovery (L2).

66
00:05:00.483 --> 00:05:04.409
Replicas diverge, so repair must be cheap to run continuously.

67
00:05:04.689 --> 00:05:08.209
Merkle trees find small differences in kilobytes (L3).

68
00:05:08.489 --> 00:05:10.323
Consistency is per request:

69
00:05:10.603 --> 00:05:16.491
R + W N gives read-your-writes, and costs you the tail of the slower replicas (L4).

70
