118
00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:02.577
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

119
00:07:03.827 --> 00:07:06.195
Intuition: a ledger, not a whiteboard.

120
00:07:06.475 --> 00:07:13.173
A whiteboard lets you erase and rewrite (update in place), but erasing is slow when the board is huge and crowded.

121
00:07:13.453 --> 00:07:18.808
A ledger only lets you append: writing is fast, and the latest line for an account wins.

122
00:07:19.088 --> 00:07:28.560
To read an account, you search from the newest pages backwards, and every so often you copy the ledger into a clean new book with only the latest lines (compaction).

123
00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.037
Clerks in three branches keep copies.

124
00:07:31.317 --> 00:07:37.931
To check they agree, they compare page totals, then line totals only on pages that differ (Merkle trees).

125
00:07:38.211 --> 00:07:43.587
How many branches must confirm before you trust a balance is up to you (R and W).

126
00:07:44.937 --> 00:07:45.897
Requirements.

127
00:07:46.177 --> 00:07:54.177
Functional. get(key), put(key, value), delete(key); optional TTL; conditional put (compare-and-set) for some keys.

128
00:07:54.457 --> 00:07:57.486
Per-request consistency level (ONE, QUORUM, ALL).

129
00:07:57.766 --> 00:08:00.710
Range scans within a partition (for timelines).

130
00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.099
Non-functional.

131
00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:11.553
Scale horizontally to tens of TB and hundreds of thousands of ops per second. p99 10 ms for QUORUM ops in-region.

132
00:08:12.903 --> 00:08:16.529
Survive node loss without data loss (RF=3).

133
00:08:16.809 --> 00:08:19.113
Keep serving during a single-AZ outage.

134
00:08:19.393 --> 00:08:23.532
Acknowledged writes are durable according to the chosen durability level.

