201
00:11:32.759 --> 00:11:37.388
The LSM tree (LevelDB, RocksDB, Cassandra, ScyllaDB):

202
00:11:37.668 --> 00:11:43.535
The write path is WAL, then memtable, then flush to immutable sorted SSTables.

203
00:11:43.815 --> 00:11:47.185
Reads check the memtable, then SSTables newest-first.

204
00:11:48.535 --> 00:11:53.997
Deletes are tombstones, which must survive until compaction has passed every older copy.

205
00:11:54.277 --> 00:11:57.093
Bloom filters make negative lookups nearly free.

206
00:11:57.373 --> 00:12:00.509
Without them, a missing key reads every SSTable.

207
00:12:00.789 --> 00:12:01.706
Compaction:

208
00:12:01.986 --> 00:12:05.421
Size-tiered (write-optimised; higher space amplification).

209
00:12:05.701 --> 00:12:09.605
Leveled (read- and space-optimised; higher write amplification).

210
00:12:09.885 --> 00:12:12.829
Time-window (for TTL or time-series data).

211
00:12:14.179 --> 00:12:18.040
It competes with foreground I or O, so it needs throttling and headroom.

212
00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:21.435
Turning it down during peaks is how the story's disks filled.

213
00:12:21.715 --> 00:12:30.824
B-trees (InnoDB, PostgreSQL) update in place: good reads and range scans, random writes, and write amplification from pages.

214
00:12:31.104 --> 00:12:37.739
The RUM conjecture says you can't minimise read, update and memory or space overheads all at once.

