249
00:14:03.197 --> 00:14:08.338
Check that the device honours flushes, and that its write cache is battery-backed where relevant.

250
00:14:08.618 --> 00:14:11.711
Group commit batches concurrent writes into one fsync.

251
00:14:11.991 --> 00:14:16.258
It is the reason databases and Kafka can be both durable and fast.

252
00:14:17.608 --> 00:14:24.605
Replication as durability: with RF=3 and W=QUORUM, a write is on two machines.

253
00:14:24.885 --> 00:14:33.397
Many stores then fsync the commit log periodically (for example, every 10 s), betting that two machines won't lose power at once.

254
00:14:33.677 --> 00:14:36.237
That is a choice to make knowingly, per keyspace.

255
00:14:36.517 --> 00:14:44.453
Recovery: replay the WAL into the memtable, and stop at the first record with a bad length or CRC (a torn write).

256
00:14:45.803 --> 00:14:47.297
Replication maintenance.

257
00:14:47.577 --> 00:14:53.827
Hinted handoff: when a replica is down, a coordinator stores the write as a hint and delivers it later.

258
00:14:54.107 --> 00:14:58.843
With a sloppy quorum, stand-in nodes accept writes to keep W satisfiable.

259
00:14:59.123 --> 00:15:02.537
That keeps writes available, but a read quorum may not see them.

260
00:15:02.817 --> 00:15:08.683
Read repair: a read that sees divergent versions writes the newest back to the stale replicas.

