351
00:18:55.567 --> 00:19:04.313
R + W N guarantees the read set overlaps the write set, so read-your-writes holds, as long as there are no sloppy-quorum stand-ins.

352
00:19:04.593 --> 00:19:07.175
Otherwise, reads can miss the client's own write.

353
00:19:07.455 --> 00:19:11.231
Tails: waiting for more replicas means inheriting more stalls.

354
00:19:11.511 --> 00:19:15.415
W=ALL makes every replica's hiccup a write outage.

355
00:19:15.695 --> 00:19:19.193
Hedging or speculative retries fix tails, not staleness.

356
00:19:19.473 --> 00:19:22.759
Sending every read to all replicas triples read load.

357
00:19:23.039 --> 00:19:24.169
Beyond quorums:

358
00:19:25.519 --> 00:19:32.815
Linearizable per-key operations (compare-and-set for idempotency keys, inventory counters) need consensus:

359
00:19:33.095 --> 00:19:40.541
Paxos or Raft, like Cassandra LWT or a Raft-based store such as etcd or TiKV (Ch 12).

360
00:19:40.821 --> 00:19:49.098
Conflicts from concurrent writes need LWW (clock-skew risk), vector clocks with siblings, or CRDTs (Ch 10).

361
00:19:50.448 --> 00:19:52.048
Partitioning and membership.

362
00:19:52.328 --> 00:19:55.528
Consistent hashing with virtual nodes (Ch 11).

363
00:19:55.808 --> 00:20:02.955
Each key's N replicas are the next N distinct nodes on the ring, spread across racks or AZs (rack-aware placement).

364
00:20:03.235 --> 00:20:10.595
Membership and failure detection: gossip with phi-accrual failure detectors (Cassandra), or a coordination service.

365
00:20:10.875 --> 00:20:14.672
A node that is suspected isn't removed; it is routed around.

366
00:20:14.952 --> 00:20:23.315
Hot partitions: a single hot key (a viral cart? unlikely; a global counter, yes) needs key splitting or a different design.

367
00:20:24.665 --> 00:20:26.243
Build, buy or configure.

368
00:20:26.523 --> 00:20:33.115
Managed (DynamoDB, Cosmos DB, Bigtable) removes storage-engine and repair operations.

369
00:20:33.395 --> 00:20:38.003
You pay per request and capacity, and keep your data-modelling responsibility.

370
00:20:38.283 --> 00:20:46.113
Self-managed (Cassandra or ScyllaDB, Riak) gives control, at the cost of operating compaction, repair and upgrades.

371
00:20:46.393 --> 00:20:55.929
Raft-based KV (etcd, TiKV, FoundationDB) gives linearizable operations and transactions at lower write throughput per key range.

372
00:20:56.209 --> 00:21:05.446
Redis is a cache or ephemeral store unless its persistence and replication settings are explicitly chosen for durability (Ch 44).

373
00:21:06.796 --> 00:21:07.927
Interview framing.

374
00:21:08.207 --> 00:21:09.828
Design a key-value store.

375
00:21:10.108 --> 00:21:10.791
Cover:

376
00:21:11.071 --> 00:21:28.095
API and consistency needs;. partitioning (consistent hashing, vnodes);. replication (N, placement);. consistency (R, W, sloppy quorum, hinted handoff);. conflict resolution;. the storage engine (LSM, compaction, Bloom filters);.

377
00:21:29.445 --> 00:21:36.954
durability (WAL, fsync, group commit);. repair (read repair, Merkle trees);. membership and failure detection.

378
00:21:37.234 --> 00:21:46.557
Staff-level signals include amplification trade-offs, ack means durable, tombstones and gcgrace, and tail latency versus consistency.

