713
00:44:03.477 --> 00:44:04.821
Interview Q and A.

714
00:44:06.071 --> 00:44:06.903
Beginner.

715
00:44:07.183 --> 00:44:08.207
Question 1.

716
00:44:08.487 --> 00:44:11.836
Design a distributed key-value store, main components?

717
00:44:12.116 --> 00:44:13.481
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:44:13.761 --> 00:44:32.983
Consistent-hash partitioning with virtual nodes; N replicas per key across racks or AZs; coordinators that apply R or W consistency levels; hinted handoff, read repair and Merkle anti-entropy; per-node LSM storage (commit log, memtable, SSTables, Bloom filters,

719
00:44:33.263 --> 00:44:41.711
compaction); gossip membership and failure detection; conflict resolution by LWW, vector clocks or CRDTs.

720
00:44:43.061 --> 00:44:44.063
Question 2.

721
00:44:44.343 --> 00:44:46.071
How does an LSM tree work?

722
00:44:46.351 --> 00:44:47.717
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:44:47.997 --> 00:45:08.327
Writes append to a commit log and update an in-memory sorted memtable; full memtables flush to immutable sorted SSTables; reads check memtable then SSTables newest-first, skipping via Bloom filters; deletes are tombstones; compaction merges SSTables, dropping old versions and expired tombstones.

724
00:45:09.677 --> 00:45:10.723
Question 3.

725
00:45:11.003 --> 00:45:13.200
What does R + W N guarantee?

726
00:45:13.480 --> 00:45:14.845
Thirty-second answer:

727
00:45:15.125 --> 00:45:28.779
Every read quorum overlaps every write quorum, so a read sees at least one replica with the latest acknowledged write (absent sloppy quorums and concurrent writes), read-your-writes; it does not make operations linearizable.

728
00:45:30.129 --> 00:45:31.195
Question 4.

729
00:45:31.475 --> 00:45:34.185
What is a Bloom filter doing in a storage engine?

730
00:45:34.465 --> 00:45:35.830
Thirty-second answer:

731
00:45:36.110 --> 00:45:51.406
Per SSTable, a compact bit array answering definitely not here or maybe here; it lets reads skip SSTables that can't contain the key, making negative lookups nearly free at ~10 bits per key for 1% false positives.

732
00:45:52.756 --> 00:45:53.759
Intermediate.

733
00:45:54.039 --> 00:45:55.148
Question 5.

734
00:45:55.428 --> 00:45:56.857
When is a write durable?

735
00:45:57.137 --> 00:45:58.503
Thirty-second answer:

736
00:45:58.783 --> 00:46:04.564
When it's on stable storage (fsynced) on as many machines as your durability promise requires.

737
00:46:04.844 --> 00:46:13.975
Process buffers die with the process, the OS page cache dies with power, fsync survives both; replication spreads risk across machines.

738
00:46:14.255 --> 00:46:16.217
Ack only after the promised level.

739
00:46:17.567 --> 00:46:18.677
Question 6.

740
00:46:18.957 --> 00:46:21.111
What are hinted handoff and read repair?

741
00:46:21.391 --> 00:46:22.757
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:46:23.037 --> 00:46:30.119
Hinted handoff: coordinators store writes for unreachable replicas and replay them when they return (within a window).

743
00:46:30.399 --> 00:46:36.309
Read repair: when a read sees divergent versions, the newest is written back to stale replicas.

744
00:46:36.589 --> 00:46:41.815
Both reduce divergence; neither guarantees full convergence, anti-entropy does.

745
00:46:43.165 --> 00:46:44.253
Question 7.

746
00:46:44.533 --> 00:46:46.432
How do Merkle trees help repair?

747
00:46:46.712 --> 00:46:48.077
Thirty-second answer:

748
00:46:48.357 --> 00:47:04.187
Each replica builds a hash tree over key ranges; comparing roots shows whether ranges match; descending only into differing subtrees locates divergent leaves; only those keys are exchanged, kilobytes for small divergence instead of whole ranges.

749
00:47:05.537 --> 00:47:06.561
Question 8.

750
00:47:06.841 --> 00:47:09.081
What are tombstones and why do they matter?

751
00:47:09.361 --> 00:47:10.726
Thirty-second answer:

752
00:47:11.006 --> 00:47:26.323
Deletes in LSM or replicated stores are markers, not removals, so older copies elsewhere don't resurrect the data; they must persist until every replica has seen them (gcgrace) and compaction can drop both marker and data.

753
00:47:26.603 --> 00:47:30.998
Too many slow reads; purging before repair completes resurrects deletes.

754
00:47:32.348 --> 00:47:33.415
Question 9.

755
00:47:33.695 --> 00:47:36.191
Bitcask versus LSM versus B-tree?

756
00:47:36.471 --> 00:47:37.836
Thirty-second answer:

757
00:47:38.116 --> 00:47:44.943
Bitcask: append log + in-memory hash index, one-seek reads, all keys in RAM, no ranges.

758
00:47:45.223 --> 00:47:51.708
LSM: sequential writes, sparse in-memory index, Bloom filters and compaction, good for write-heavy.

759
00:47:51.988 --> 00:47:57.940
B-tree: in-place updates, great reads and ranges, random writes and page write amplification.

760
00:47:59.290 --> 00:48:00.058
Senior.

761
00:48:00.338 --> 00:48:01.383
Question 10.

762
00:48:01.663 --> 00:48:04.479
How do you choose consistency levels per operation?

763
00:48:04.759 --> 00:48:06.125
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:48:06.405 --> 00:48:27.738
By what a stale or lost read or write costs: carts and order status need read-your-writes (QUORUM or QUORUM); sessions and counters tolerate ONE; compare-and-set needs consensus; consider tail latency (quorums inherit the second-slowest replica), availability during failures, and hedging or speculative retry for tails.

765
00:48:29.088 --> 00:48:30.219
Question 11.

766
00:48:30.499 --> 00:48:33.293
How does compaction strategy affect a workload?

767
00:48:33.573 --> 00:48:34.939
Thirty-second answer:

768
00:48:35.219 --> 00:48:43.240
Size-tiered merges similar-sized files: low write amplification, high space or read amplification, good for write-heavy.

769
00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:52.459
Leveled keeps non-overlapping levels: reads touch ~1 file per level, low space amp, higher write amp, good for read-heavy or updates.

770
00:48:52.739 --> 00:48:56.813
Time-window groups by time for TTL data, dropping whole files.

771
00:48:58.163 --> 00:48:59.251
Question 12.

772
00:48:59.531 --> 00:49:01.835
How do you handle a node replacement safely?

773
00:49:02.115 --> 00:49:03.481
Thirty-second answer:

774
00:49:03.761 --> 00:49:17.478
Bootstrap the new node to stream its ranges from replicas (throttled), keep QUORUM consistency during streaming, run repair after, verify with Merkle roots, avoid doing it at peak, and ensure disk headroom on neighbours.

775
00:49:18.828 --> 00:49:20.108
Question 13.

776
00:49:20.388 --> 00:49:23.225
How do you implement compare-and-set in a leaderless store?

777
00:49:23.505 --> 00:49:24.871
Thirty-second answer:

778
00:49:25.151 --> 00:49:28.884
You can't with quorums alone, concurrent writers can both succeed.

779
00:49:29.164 --> 00:49:40.513
Use a consensus protocol per key (Paxos-based lightweight transactions in Cassandra, Raft in etcd or TiKV) at higher latency, or route the key to a single writer.

780
00:49:41.863 --> 00:49:43.058
Question 14.

781
00:49:43.338 --> 00:49:45.663
What metrics reveal storage engine trouble?

782
00:49:45.943 --> 00:49:47.309
Thirty-second answer:

783
00:49:47.589 --> 00:50:00.517
Pending compactions, SSTables per read, Bloom false-positive rates, tombstones scanned per read, disk usage versus headroom, write stalls (memtable flush backpressure), p99 by operation.

784
00:50:01.867 --> 00:50:02.763
Architect.

785
00:50:03.043 --> 00:50:04.237
Question 15.

786
00:50:04.517 --> 00:50:07.653
Design a Dynamo-style key-value store end to end.

787
00:50:07.933 --> 00:50:09.299
Thirty-second answer:

788
00:50:09.579 --> 00:50:33.131
Ring with vnodes, rack-aware replicas, coordinator with per-request R or W, sloppy quorum + hinted handoff, read repair, Merkle anti-entropy, vector clocks or LWW or CRDT conflict handling, LSM storage with group-committed WAL, Bloom filters and tuned compaction, gossip membership with phi detectors, monitoring and repair scheduling.

789
00:50:34.481 --> 00:50:35.675
Question 16.

790
00:50:35.955 --> 00:50:39.262
Managed (DynamoDB) or self-managed (Cassandra)?

791
00:50:39.542 --> 00:50:40.907
Thirty-second answer:

792
00:50:41.187 --> 00:50:54.094
Managed removes operations (compaction, repair, upgrades) and scales with pay-per-use; self-managed gives control, predictable cost at steady high throughput, and portability, at the price of expertise.

793
00:50:54.374 --> 00:50:59.409
Decide by team capacity, cost at your load, feature needs and lock-in tolerance.

794
00:51:00.759 --> 00:51:02.103
Question 17.

795
00:51:02.383 --> 00:51:06.735
How do you migrate carts from PostgreSQL to a KV store safely?

796
00:51:07.015 --> 00:51:08.380
Thirty-second answer:

797
00:51:08.660 --> 00:51:23.039
Model by access pattern; dual-write with the old store authoritative; backfill; compare reads (shadow reads) and fix differences; switch reads per cohort; switch authority; keep a rollback path; monitor consistency and latency.

798
00:51:24.389 --> 00:51:25.562
Question 18.

799
00:51:25.842 --> 00:51:29.127
What happens to a leaderless store during a network partition?

800
00:51:29.407 --> 00:51:30.773
Thirty-second answer:

801
00:51:31.053 --> 00:51:49.314
Each side may accept writes if it can form the requested quorum (or sloppy quorum); after healing, conflicting versions are reconciled by LWW (possible lost updates), siblings or vector clocks (app merge), or CRDTs; hints and repair converge replicas.

802
00:51:49.594 --> 00:51:51.834
QUORUM writes fail on the minority side.

803
00:51:53.184 --> 00:51:54.421
Question 19.

804
00:51:54.701 --> 00:51:56.515
How do you size a KV cluster?

805
00:51:56.795 --> 00:51:58.160
Thirty-second answer:

806
00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:20.477
From data times RF times space amplification times headroom (≥ 2 times for compaction), IOPS for reads or writes including compaction write amplification, latency targets with consistency levels, and failure tolerance (serve with one AZ down); validate with load tests including compaction and repair running.

807
00:52:21.827 --> 00:52:22.937
Question 20.

808
00:52:23.217 --> 00:52:25.798
What does 'healthy' look like for the KV platform?

809
00:52:26.078 --> 00:52:27.443
Thirty-second answer:

810
00:52:27.723 --> 00:52:44.107
Latency within SLO per consistency level; no dropped mutations; bounded pending compactions and SSTables per read; disk under headroom; repair completing within gcgrace; hints small; zero acknowledged-write loss in chaos tests.

811
00:52:45.457 --> 00:52:46.353
Part 9.

812
00:52:46.633 --> 00:52:48.041
Summary and Practice.

813
00:52:49.291 --> 00:52:50.379
Key takeaways.

814
00:52:50.659 --> 00:52:55.289
LSM trees trade read and space amplification for sequential writes.

815
00:52:55.569 --> 00:52:58.449
Bloom filters and compaction pay it back (L1).

816
00:52:58.729 --> 00:53:03.251
Ack means durable: fsync, or group commit, before acknowledging (L2).

817
00:53:03.531 --> 00:53:05.985
Merkle trees make routine repair cheap.

818
00:53:06.265 --> 00:53:08.846
Large divergence means streaming (L3).

819
00:53:09.126 --> 00:53:13.137
R + W N gives read-your-writes and costs tail latency.

820
00:53:13.417 --> 00:53:16.297
Consensus is needed for CAS (L4).

821
00:53:16.577 --> 00:53:20.353
Operations are the product: headroom, repair and tombstones.

822
00:53:21.703 --> 00:53:23.025
Decision cheat sheet.

823
00:53:23.305 --> 00:53:24.649
Here is a quick table:

824
00:53:24.929 --> 00:53:26.103
Need, and Choice.

825
00:53:26.383 --> 00:53:27.812
Write-heavy per-key data:

826
00:53:28.092 --> 00:53:32.145
LSM (leveled for updates, TWCS for TTL).

827
00:53:32.425 --> 00:53:33.599
Negative lookups:

828
00:53:33.879 --> 00:53:36.375
Bloom filters ~1% FP.

829
00:53:36.655 --> 00:53:37.700
Durable acks:

830
00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.177
Group-committed WAL with fsync.

831
00:53:40.457 --> 00:53:41.844
Replica convergence:

832
00:53:42.124 --> 00:53:45.516
Hints + read repair + incremental Merkle repair.

833
00:53:45.796 --> 00:53:46.863
Read-your-writes:

834
00:53:47.143 --> 00:53:48.316
QUORUM or QUORUM.

835
00:53:48.596 --> 00:53:49.321
CAS:

836
00:53:49.601 --> 00:53:52.033
Consensus (LWT or Raft).

837
00:53:52.313 --> 00:53:53.167
Tails:

838
00:53:53.447 --> 00:53:55.431
Speculative retry or hedging.

839
00:53:56.781 --> 00:53:57.911
Exercises.

840
00:53:58.191 --> 00:54:02.245
(B2) In L1, flush at 16 MB instead of 4 MB.

841
00:54:02.525 --> 00:54:05.810
How do SSTable count and read amplification change?

842
00:54:06.090 --> 00:54:10.314
(B3) In L2, change the group-commit batch to wait up to 2 ms.

843
00:54:10.594 --> 00:54:13.090
Measure acks per second and ack latency.

844
00:54:13.370 --> 00:54:17.061
(B3) In L3, use 4-way fanout instead of binary.

845
00:54:17.341 --> 00:54:19.026
Compare bytes and round trips.

846
00:54:19.306 --> 00:54:24.511
(B4) In L4, add a sloppy quorum: during a stall a stand-in accepts the write.

847
00:54:24.791 --> 00:54:27.522
Does QUORUM still give 0% stale reads?

848
00:54:28.872 --> 00:54:35.741
(B4) Design leveled compaction and compute its write amplification for 10 levels with a ratio of 10.

